## Emma

1. The student describes/motivates societal relevance of the main research question (societal relevance).

The research is well-motivated, placing the topic of the Roman empire in the perspective of a broader historical persistence literature. The motivation to look at poverty specifically, however, is a little bit ad hoc. In this regard, more elaborate attempts to place this into a more general development context could have been made. 

2. The student describes the contribution to the existing literature (scientific relevance).

Several important points in terms of methodology are addressed, and the results and approach of the present study are contrasted and compared with the approaches of other studies. In addition, this study explicitly debates with previous studies addressing similar questions. 

3. The student motivates and explains the research approach (methodology)

The research approach and methodological assumptions are explained quite clearly. The historical background is discussed extensively, and, impressively, there are also explicit links between the historical background and the econometric assumptions underlying the research design. 

4. The student applies the research approach in an appropriate and correct way; if applicable,  insights and theories of the dedicated minor are incorporated in the research (approach).

