## Emilio

1. The student describes/motivates societal relevance of the main research question (societal relevance).

The introduction is well-written, but relatively scarce attention is paid to societal relevance. It is introducing the setting as a case study, and not really elaborating on the importance of answering this research question from a societal perspective. 

2. The student describes the contribution to the existing literature (scientific relevance).

There is a large body of literature being referred to: in addition to empirical studies, the study also proceeds from a more theoretical angle. On the other hand, some of the explanations are quite brief, and the text could have benefited from more extensive considerations. In some cases, the literature referred to is also a bit ad hoc, and the relevance to the present study is not fully clear. 

3. The student motivates and explains the research approach (methodology)

The theoretical framework is a special case of the 2SLS-framework. In general, the assumptions are laid bare in a transparent way, and the assumptions are backed up using relevant case studies and other research. There are some subtle mistakes in spelling out the precise formulae, but in general, the approach is correct, and written out in a detailed way. There is no real justification for using distance to road, as explained only later in the study, and only including municipalities with a road and their neighbours. 

4. The student applies the research approach in an appropriate and correct way; if applicable,  insights and theories of the dedicated minor are incorporated in the research (approach).

The results are analyzed more or less correctly, but the way in which the results are reported is sometimes difficult to oversee - whereas the theoretical framework is clear, the empirical approach sometimes features unexpected additions to the design which are difficult to oversee. The regression tables are not idiomatic and it is difficult to read them. Also, the differences between treatment and instrument are conflated in some tables and text. 

5. The student interprets research findings in the light of societal and scientific relevance (interpretation).

The study returns to the reuslts in the conclusion, and discusses them in light of theory. There is an extensive discussion of the limitations. 

