## Jonathan

1. The student describes/motivates societal relevance of the main research question (societal relevance).

The societal relevance is clearly motivated, with the help of anecdotes and with the help of literature, although the introduction is written in a slightly ad hoc way, and starts off too general. 

2. The student describes the contribution to the existing literature (scientific relevance).

The scientific literature is discussed, but it is only focusing on Roman roads and does not pay enough attention to the more general question of infrastructural persistence and the economic mechanisms underlying it. In the introduction, some studies are mentioned, but the mechanisms could have been elaborated upon more extensively. 

3. The student motivates and explains the research approach (methodology)

The data sources are described extensively, and there is an overview of variabels included in the analysis. This gives a lot of oversight. Also, some original data sources have been used, even though the elaboration on the details is quite limited. There is, however, no equation and a definition of some central variables used in the analysis. 

4. The student applies the research approach in an appropriate and correct way; if applicable,  insights and theories of the dedicated minor are incorporated in the research (approach).

An extensive body of analyses is conducted, exploring both the intensive and extensive margins and various fixed effects. The resulting estimates are very stable and point to a very clear persistence influence of Roman roads. The mechanisms section is a little bit confusing, as it presents consequences of Roman roads rather than intermediate factors investigating the persistence. On the other hand, section 4.4 discusses the persistence over time by making use of an original dataset on roads in France in the 19th century. 

5. The student interprets research findings in the light of societal and scientific relevance (interpretation).

The results are discussed in light of societal issues such as pollution, and a standardized analysis is also employed to compare the effects of persistence nowadays with persistence up until the 19th century. This analysis show that the persistent effects have become stronger, rather than weaker, indicating that economic development (or at least infrastructural development) is very path dependent. 

6. The student presents his/her research in an appropriate and structured way (written presentation).

The research is presented in an appropriate and structured way. 

7. The student conducted the research in an independent and self-responsible way (process).

The research was conducted in an independent and autonomous way. 

