## Diana

1. The student describes/motivates societal relevance of the main research question (societal relevance).

The introduction is well-written and clearly focuses on societal relevance. The research is placed in the context of a wider debate about migration in Europe. In addition, various claims are being backed up by references from diverse literatures. 

2. The student describes the contribution to the existing literature (scientific relevance).

The literature is embedded in the introduction, which is mainly focused on societal relevance - this way, the scientific relevance does not come enough to the foreground. The literature section confines itself to mentioning the results without discussing explicitly the theoretical mechanisms at hand. 

3. The student motivates and explains the research approach (methodology)

The research approach is explained clearly in the methodology esction. Some of the graphs are kind of ad hoc, but fortunately, there is a clear table containing the descriptive statistics. Control variables are also discussed. However, the methodology is postponed to the results section, which does not contribute to a clear structure.

4. The student applies the research approach in an appropriate and correct way; if applicable,  insights and theories of the dedicated minor are incorporated in the research (approach).

The results are presented in a somewhat chaotic way - the approach implied in the methodology is a fixed effects approach, but the tables seem to show logit regressions. It is not clear how (country) fixed effects are or might be implemented in this setting. On the other hand, different methods seem to be employed, which adds to the robustness of the results, but it is difficult to retain oversight due to the non-standard format of reporting.

5. The student interprets research findings in the light of societal and scientific relevance (interpretation).

The interpretation focuses on societal relevance and makes clear that the study is aware of the limitations with respect to drawing causal conclusions. It also explicitly discussing potential confounding variables and reasons why the results might not be causal. On the other hand, concrete policy recommendations are not really discussed. 

